.TH proxyto 1 "September 2026" "saphira-proxyto" "Saphira Manual"
.SH NAME
proxyto \- small generic TCP compatibility proxy for the HAProxy PROXY protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proxyto
.RB [ \-\-syslog ]
.I name
.br
.B proxyto
.RB [ \-\-syslog ]
.B \-c
.I /path/to/config.conf
.br
.B proxyto
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B proxyto
is a small generic TCP compatibility proxy.
It accepts connections from an upstream proxy speaking the HAProxy PROXY
protocol version 1 or version 2, removes the PROXY metadata, then makes a
normal TCP connection to an application which does not understand the PROXY
protocol and relays the byte stream unchanged in both directions.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Internet client
      |
    HAProxy
   PROXY v2
      |
    proxyto
   clean TCP
      |
   Geomyidae
.fi
.RE
.PP
Geomyidae receives ordinary Gopher bytes. It never sees the PROXY header.
.PP
.B proxyto
itself retains the original client metadata and can therefore log the real
source address.
.PP
Although Geomyidae/Gopher is the first use case,
.B proxyto
is not specific to Gopher or to HAProxy.
Any ordinary TCP application that cannot consume PROXY protocol can sit
behind it. No Gopher-specific, HTTP-specific or TLS-specific code is
included.
.SH SAPHIRA LINUX
Saphira Linux is a small Linux distribution built around
.BR musl libc ,
explicit package ownership and conventional Unix system administration.
It favours small components with clearly defined jobs, ordinary
configuration files, standard process supervision and infrastructure that
remains under the operator's control.
.PP
Saphira systems are intended to remain understandable from the shell:
configuration is local, processes are visible, logs are ordinary system
logs, and services can be assembled from independent components rather
than requiring an external control plane.
Configuration and logs remain on systems the operator controls, using
standard Unix interfaces, ordinary files and processes, and
operator-controlled infrastructure with no external control plane required.
.PP
You run it; you configure it; you can inspect it; it belongs to your
infrastructure.
.SH WHY PROXYTO EXISTS
Many TCP proxies can preserve an original client's IP address by adding a
PROXY protocol header to the beginning of a connection.
.PP
An application which understands PROXY protocol consumes that header and
uses the enclosed source and destination addresses.
.PP
An application which does not understand it instead sees the header as
application data. For example a connection that should begin with a Gopher
selector may begin with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PROXY TCP4 192.0.2.10 192.0.2.20 43122 70\\r\\n
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a Gopher server this is interpreted as a Gopher selector and the
request is therefore wrong. The same class of problem affects any
protocol that does not expect a prepended metadata line.
.PP
.B proxyto
provides the boundary:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PROXY-aware infrastructure -> proxyto -> ordinary TCP application
.fi
.RE
.PP
This avoids carrying application-specific patches merely to teach every
small TCP daemon about PROXY protocol.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B proxyto
is normally invoked with a single name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
proxyto geomyidae
.fi
.RE
.PP
which loads:
.PP
.RS
.I /etc/saphira/proxyto/geomyidae.conf
.RE
.PP
An explicit path may be used for testing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
proxyto \-c /path/to/custom.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Configuration is a small key=value file. Lines starting with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments. Whitespace around keys and values is trimmed.
An example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
application=/usr/bin/geomyidae
listen=172.16.0.2:70
proxy=127.0.0.1:70
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TP
.B application=
Descriptive and validation information. The path to the program
associated with this proxy definition. Currently informational only;
.B proxyto
does not start, stop or supervise the program. If the path exists but
is not executable a warning is logged. OpenRC or systemd owns process
supervision.
.TP
.B listen=
The exact local IP address and TCP port on which
.B proxyto
accepts PROXY-protocol connections. The address is bound exactly as
written; there is no fallback to
.B 0.0.0.0 .
The listener is explicitly a PROXY-protocol listener.
.TP
.B proxy=
The backend TCP destination to which the clean application stream is
forwarded after the PROXY header has been removed.
.TP
.B trusted_proxy=
Optional comma-separated list of upstream IP addresses that are allowed
to connect to the
.B listen
socket. When set, only peers whose source IP matches an entry are
accepted; all others are rejected before any PROXY header is processed
and a message such as
.I peer=192.0.2.99:54321 rejected (untrusted proxy)
is logged. When not set, any peer may connect and the listener should
still be protected by host firewall policy. Up to 32 entries. Each
entry is a literal IPv4 or IPv6 address, optionally bracketed for
IPv6, for example:
.RS
.nf
trusted_proxy=203.0.113.10
trusted_proxy=203.0.113.10, 203.0.113.11
trusted_proxy=203.0.113.10, [2001:db8::1]
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B header_timeout=
Milliseconds to wait for a complete PROXY header before rejecting
the connection (100..300000, default 5000). Corresponds to haproxy's
client-side request timeout concept.
.TP
.B connect_timeout=
Milliseconds to wait for the backend TCP connect (100..300000,
default 5000). Corresponds to haproxy's timeout connect.
.TP
.B buffer_size=
Per-direction relay buffer in bytes (4096..1048576, default 16384,
matching haproxy's tune.bufsize). Larger pipelined tails that exceed
it are forwarded by direct write instead of buffering.
.TP
.B max_children=
Maximum concurrent workers (1..4096, default 256). Each accepted
connection forks; at the cap the daemon refuses new connections
instead of queueing them, so a peer that can reach the listener cannot
exhaust PIDs, fds, or memory. Saturation is logged once on entry and
once on recovery, not once per connection.
.TP
.B trusted_proxy=
Comma-separated list of source addresses permitted to assert an
identity. May appear only once: a repeated definition is an error
rather than a silent overwrite. Must be present and non-empty, or the
daemon refuses to start.
.TP
.B service_type=
Backend service label (1..63 chars of letters, digits and
.B _ - . + ,
default
.BR generic ).
Used in log lines; reserves the namespace for future per-service
relay tuning. Examples:
.BR gopher ,
.BR postgres ,
.BR ssh .
.PP
Identical port numbers on different addresses are valid, because they
refer to different sockets:
.PP
.RS
.nf
listen=172.16.0.2:70
proxy=127.0.0.1:70
.fi
.RE
.PP
IPv6 must use brackets to avoid ambiguity between colons in the
address and the port separator:
.PP
.RS
.nf
listen=[2a02:8012:bc57:1::10]:70
proxy=[::1]:70
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bare IPv6 without brackets (for example
.I 2a02:8012:bc57:1::10:70
) is rejected as ambiguous.
.SH PROXY PROTOCOL
The implementation follows the vendored and current HAProxy PROXY
protocol specification
.RI ( proxy\-protocol.txt ).
.PP
Supported:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
PROXY protocol version 1 (human-readable, single line terminated by CRLF)
.IP \(bu 2
PROXY protocol version 2 (binary, with 12-byte signature, version/command,
family/protocol and 16-bit length)
.IP \(bu 2
TCP over IPv4 and TCP over IPv6
.IP \(bu 2
.B LOCAL
and
.B UNKNOWN
semantics
.RE
.PP
Other families (for example UNIX sockets or UDP) are accepted and
skipped via the
.B UNSPEC
fallback where applicable, but are not used for TCP relay.
.PP
The complete PROXY header is consumed before any application data is
forwarded. Bytes that arrived in the same segment after the header are
retained and forwarded as the first payload bytes. Malformed,
incomplete or unsupported headers cause the connection to be rejected.
A plain TCP connection sent to a PROXY-only listener is rejected.
No attempt is made to guess whether PROXY protocol is in use; the
listener expects it unconditionally. Partial header reads are
reassembled incrementally with a timeout (see
.BR header_timeout ),
including headers arriving in small TCP segments.
.SH PRIVILEGES
The daemon starts as root only to bind privileged ports. After the
listening socket exists it permanently drops to the dedicated
.B proxyto
account (supplementary groups, gid, uid, in that order) and verifies
the drop cannot be regained; any failure is fatal and the daemon
refuses to continue, never as root and never as a backend
application's account. All accepted connections and PROXY parsing
therefore run unprivileged. Started unprivileged (tests, manual
runs), it logs a notice and continues, there being nothing to drop.
The runtime account must exist before a privileged start.
.SH AUTHORITY
PROXY protocol version 2 can carry Type-Length-Value (TLV) extensions
after the address block. Type
.B 0x02
is
.BR PP2_TYPE_AUTHORITY .
It transports a host name or authority value supplied by the upstream
proxy, as an opaque byte sequence intended to be UTF-8. The common case
is that this value originates from the TLS Server Name Indication (SNI)
extension, but the field is more general: the HAProxy specification
explicitly notes that authority can exist without TLS. Other sources
include a distinct frontend or virtual IP that statically implies a
hostname, or explicit load-balancer configuration.
.PP
.B proxyto
v1 parses
.B PP2_TYPE_AUTHORITY
when present, validates that it contains no embedded NUL or ASCII
control characters, retains it as a bounded string suitable for logging
and future matching, and logs it.
.PP
It does not currently:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
route to different backends based on authority,
.IP \(bu 2
inject authority or an invented hostname into the backend byte stream,
.IP \(bu 2
invent a hostname where none was supplied.
.RE
.PP
A plain backend such as Geomyidae therefore continues to receive
untouched Gopher. For version 1 connections, or version 2 connections
with no AUTHORITY TLV, the configured
.B proxy=
destination is used unchanged. Authority-aware routing is reserved for
a future extension and will not require a protocol change because the
value is already retained.
.SH LOGGING
.B proxyto
knows the real client address from the PROXY header and logs it.
.PP
On connect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
proxyto[1234]: geomyidae client=[2001:db8::10]:43122 backend=127.0.0.1:70 connected authority=gopher.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.I authority=
field appears only when a v2 AUTHORITY TLV was supplied. Other TLVs
such as ALPN or UNIQUE_ID may be logged similarly when present.
.PP
On close:
.PP
.RS
.nf
proxyto[1234]: geomyidae client=[2001:db8::10]:43122 closed rx=42 tx=1937
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I rx
is the number of payload bytes forwarded from client to backend;
.I tx
is the number of bytes forwarded from backend to client. The PROXY
header itself is not counted.
.PP
Logging is to standard error by default, so OpenRC and systemd can
collect it as ordinary service output. The option
.B \-\-syslog
deliberately redirects logging to
.BR syslog (3)
instead (facility
.BR LOG_DAEMON ),
rather than duplicating every line to both destinations.
.PP
Startup and configuration errors are also logged in the same way.
.SH SECURITY
PROXY metadata represents trusted upstream information. The
.B listen
socket should normally only be reachable by the trusted load balancer or
proxy, typically enforced by host firewall policy. The
.B trusted_proxy
option provides an additional in-application check: when set, the source
IP of the incoming TCP connection is compared against the listed
addresses and unlisted peers are rejected before any header is consumed.
.PP
.B proxyto
validates and bounds-checks the header. It rejects malformed,
truncated, over-long or NUL/control-containing headers and enforces
field sizes and the 16-bit v2 length. It does not use unbounded string
functions on header data.
.PP
Validation cannot determine whether a claimed source address is
truthful when an untrusted host can connect directly to the listener.
Only the upstream's ability to reach the listener should be trusted to
supply accurate client information.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
No TLS termination. No application-protocol parsing.
.IP \(bu 2
No transparent source-address spoofing or TPROXY. The backend sees
.B proxyto
as its TCP peer (for example
.IR 127.0.0.1 ).
The original address is available in
.B proxyto
logs.
.IP \(bu 2
Authority is logged but does not select backends in v1.
.IP \(bu 2
The CRC32C TLV
.RB ( 0x03 )
is recognised and skipped but not verified in v1.
.IP \(bu 2
SSL TLVs
.RB ( 0x20
and subtypes) are recognised and skipped, not interpreted.
.IP \(bu 2
No SIGHUP configuration reload. Restart the service to pick up a new
configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
.B proxyto
does not supervise the backend application.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
The first deployment is Geomyidae, which explains the purpose well:
.PP
.RS
.nf
HAProxy :70
    send-proxy-v2
        |
proxyto 172.16.0.2:70
        |
geomyidae 127.0.0.1:70
.fi
.RE
.PP
HAProxy can retain the original client metadata, proxyto can log it,
and Geomyidae receives exactly the Gopher request it expects.
.PP
Minimal configuration
.RI ( /etc/saphira/proxyto/geomyidae.conf ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
application=/usr/bin/geomyidae
listen=172.16.0.2:70
proxy=127.0.0.1:70
# optional allow-list
# trusted_proxy=203.0.113.10, 203.0.113.11
.fi
.RE
.PP
Invocation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
proxyto geomyidae
.fi
.RE
.PP
Supervision (OpenRC or systemd) starts both
.B proxyto
and
.B geomyidae
as separate services.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/saphira/proxyto/*.conf
Per-service configuration files. The name
.I geomyidae
maps to
.IR geomyidae.conf .
.TP
.I /usr/bin/proxyto
The proxy binary.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/proxyto.1
This manual page.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful execution. The listener was started. Workers exit 0 on
clean relay completion.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, bind or fatal runtime error. Individual connections that
are rejected or fail to connect to the backend cause the worker to exit
non-zero, while the listener continues to serve other connections.
